definition:
    Hungarian-American theoretical physicist (1902–1995) who won the 1963 Nobel Prize in Physics for his work on the theory of the atomic nucleus and elementary particles, especially symmetry principles. A Manhattan Project veteran, he led the design team behind the Hanford plutonium-production reactors and co-invented the pressurized water reactor with Alvin Weinberg. He also described the 'Wigner effect' — neutron damage to graphite — and applied group theory so deeply to physics that colleagues called him 'the Silent Genius'.
